    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i feel like i've hit a wall. like, a literal ceiling. everything i've made lately just feels like a cheap imitation of who i used to be. it's actually painful to even pick up my instrument now because i know i'm not that person anymore. i'm just watching the decline in real time.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is incredibly common to feel a sense of hollowness when your hard work no longer feels like it \"matters,\" and that pain you feel about the \"cheap imitation\" of yourself is a painful but normal signal that you are evolving. Trying to reject a new version of yourself because it no longer matches your past self often leads to self-sabotage, so I encourage you to try allowing a gentle self-compassion to ease this self-criticism.\nInstead of trying to \"stop the decline\" or force yourself to be \"that person again,\" consider that this feeling of decline is just a painful form of growth as you discover a deeper self.\nGive yourself permission to take a break or simply listen to the pain without judgment, because the emotions you feel are real even if the direction they lead them is not always what you hope.\nYou might find that \"hitting a ceiling\" is just a bump in the road before you realize that you are now ready to build a new foundation for your creativity on more solid ground.",
